            <div xml:id="d687">

                <pb facs="0638" n="631" xml:id="pg631"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/10803: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Tsingtao</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sokobin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tsingtao</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-22T15:00:00+08:00">October 22, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 22—1:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Mayor of Tsingtao invited me this morning to discuss the situation in
                    Tsinanfu, the capital of Shantung. He referred to numerous current rumors that
                    an agreement had been reached between Han Fu Chu and Japan executives [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">military?</hi>], that a Peace Preservation Commission
                    had been established in Tsinanfu which if true would commit Shantung to a
                    separate policy and freedom of action in north. The Mayor went at length to
                    explain that he had denied negotiation of such an agreement with Japan and had
                    “severely rebuked” the leaders of the Peace Preservation Commission movement. It
                    appears to the Consulate that the Mayor’s elaborate explanation was intended to
                    cover what was probably already a <hi rendition="#italic">fait accompli</hi>,
                    that is a Han-Japanese understanding in regard to Shantung and a movement in
                    Tsingtao [for?] separation from Nanking in the politico-military sphere.</p>
                <p>Sent to Nanking, Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sokobin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
